          "What distinguishes mangroves from terrestrial rainforests is not merely rapid tree growth but extraordinary below-ground storage. Tangled arching prop roots slow incoming tidal currents and trap nutrient-rich silt carried from rivers. Because these coastal sediments remain permanently waterlogged and largely devoid of oxygen, microbial decomposition proceeds exceedingly slowly. As a result, layers of dead roots and leaf litter accumulate for centuries, locking three to five times more carbon per hectare than inland tropical forests.",
          "Beyond climate regulation, healthy mangrove belts provide indispensable economic and physical protection. Their dense root networks attenuate storm-surge waves during tropical cyclones, reducing erosion and property damage in nearby coastal towns. Simultaneously, the sheltered channels beneath the roots serve as vital nurseries for juvenile fish, shrimp, and crabs that sustain regional fisheries.",
          "Despite these advantages, coastal reclamation and conversion into aquaculture ponds have eliminated vast tracts of mangroves worldwide. When a development project drains these wetlands, oxygen infiltrates the buried sediment and triggers rapid decay, turning them from long-term carbon sinks into major greenhouse-gas emitters. Recent restoration programs therefore emphasize restoring natural tidal water flow alongside community-managed ecotourism."
