          "During the Age of Exploration, navigators could calculate latitude—north-south position—by measuring the angle of the noon Sun or North Star above the horizon. Determining longitude, or east-west position, proved far more elusive. Without a reliable method at sea, captains relied on rough speed and compass estimates, a perilously imprecise practice behind disasters like the 1707 Scilly naval wreck.",
          "Alarmed by mounting losses, the British Parliament enacted the Longitude Act of 1714, offering 20,000 pounds for any method accurate within half a degree after an Atlantic voyage. Leading astronomers championed the lunar-distance technique, tracking the Moon against background stars with intricate tables. By contrast, a self-taught Yorkshire carpenter named John Harrison proposed building a rugged mechanical clock able to preserve home-port time across months at sea.",
          "Building such an instrument defied eighteenth-century horology. Ordinary pendulum clocks failed on pitching decks, while humidity and temperature swings expanded or contracted metal balance springs, disrupting accuracy. Over three decades of experimentation, Harrison pioneered bimetallic temperature compensators and low-friction bearings, culminating in his compact pocket-sized marine watch, known as H4, in 1759.",
          "During an eighty-one-day trial to Jamaica, H4 lost merely five seconds—roughly one nautical mile and far superior to Parliament's threshold. Yet skeptical astronomers on the Board of Longitude delayed recognizing H4 for years, insisting a single triumph might be accidental; only after skilled watchmakers replicated it did marine chronometers transform global shipping and cartography."
