          "Beneath an icy shell estimated to be 15 to 25 kilometers thick lies a global saltwater ocean that may hold twice as much water as all of Earth's oceans combined. Because sunlight at Jupiter's distance is far too feeble to melt ice, scientists attribute this liquid reservoir to tidal flexing. As Europa follows a slightly elliptical orbit around Jupiter, the giant planet's immense gravity stretches and compresses the moon, generating internal heat through friction.",
          "Equally compelling is the probability that Europa's ocean rests directly atop a rocky seafloor rather than a layer of high-pressure ice. If hydrothermal vents exist along this boundary, they could circulate dissolved minerals and chemical energy into the dark water, mirroring deep-sea ecosystems on Earth that thrive without photosynthesis.",
