          "Before the mid-twentieth century, maritime freight moved primarily as break-bulk cargo—individual crates, barrels, and sacks hoisted onto ships one bundle at a time. Loading or unloading a single vessel often required hundreds of dockworkers laboring for more than a week. Because port handling was slow, pilferage was rampant, and freight spent nearly half its journey sitting idle at congested piers.",
          "In 1956, American trucking entrepreneur Malcom McLean transformed logistics by placing detachable metal trailer boxes directly onto a converted oil tanker. Rather than unpacking goods at every transfer point between trucks, trains, and ships, workers moved sealed boxes intact using mechanized cranes. Within a decade, international standards bodies adopted uniform dimensions for intermodal containers, enabling seamless transfers across continents.",
          "The economic repercussions were profound. Port turnaround times plunged from days to mere hours, and maritime shipping costs fell by more than seventy percent. As transportation ceased to be a prohibitive barrier, manufacturers could split complex supply chains across multiple countries, sourcing raw materials in one region, assembling components in another, and selling finished goods worldwide.",
